{"t":1788714152,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"a32eebe5-1ee6-478a-b9c9-7a816a59d290","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-06T14:11:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 061411\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n1011 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nAdded a bit more detail in the tides/coastal flood section. Also\nmade some edits to other sections to reflect current conditions. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures and lower\n  humidity today into early parts of the week.\n\n- 2) Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures \nand lower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic through Monday. This will bring a change in \nairmass with notably lower dew points. Highs in the low 80s for \nmost today and Monday. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid- \nAtlantic on Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the\nsoutheast CONUS. Temperatures likely begin to creep up again \nduring this time, accompanied by increasing humidity as mid-\nlevel dry air tracks south of the region with more humid air \ncoming in from the north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid \nweek.\n\nUpper-level ridging over the Appalachians starts to flatten into \nzonal flow beginning Wed. As this occurs, a trough forming over\nQuebec will begin to track east across the northeast CONUS, \nwith a stemming cold front moving across the East Coast by\nThursday. Temps gradually increase during this time, along with\nincreasing humidity ahead of the front. By Thursday, temps \npotentially reach the lower 90s across the metros. \n\nAs this frontal boundary tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, additional \nshower & thunderstorm activity is possible across the region \nbeginning Wed evening and into Thursday. As of the 00Z model \nruns, the GFS and ECMWF both indicate a Thu afternoon time of \narrival for most precip, but the exact coverage and nature of \nprecip remains uncertain. Some diurnal thunderstorm development\nremains possible during this period given the increasing \ninstability in the atmosphere by this time.\n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore towards the end of next week,\ntemps drop again & conditions become much drier heading into \nthe weekend as high pressure moves in overhead.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /14Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nLight showers and drizzle linger near CHO this morning, and will\nlikely continue to do so over the next couple hours. This should\ntaper off into the early afternoon. \n\nThereafter, VFR conditions should persist through Wednesday \nevening.\n\nShowers & thunderstorms associated with an incoming cold front \ncould temporarily bring sub-VFR conditions to terminals by\nThursday afternoon. Ahead of the front on Thursday, winds could\ngust between 20-25 knots before diminishing overnight. \nSoutherly winds shift northeasterly by Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNo marine hazards are expected through the holiday weekend. \nGenerally light flow is expected, though onshore gusts could \napproach marginal SCA levels late this afternoon. Currently\nfavoring a Marine Weather Statement given brief duration.\n\nNortheasterly winds Monday shift southerly on Tuesday, \nremaining below SCA levels.\n\nSCAs are possible across the Bay by Wed evening ahead of an \nincoming cold front. Winds could remain elevated throughout \nThu as this frontal boundary tracks east across the coast. \nsoutherly winds shift northeasterly Thu night in the wake of \nthe front.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies remain elevated over the next several days due\nto an onshore flow. Minor tidal flooding is becoming\nincreasingly likely at Annapolis, and could also be possible in\nthe other sensitive tidal locations in the northern Chesapeake \nBay. Coastal Flood Advisories will need to be considered in the\nnext 12 to 18 hours, but would like to monitor forecast trends a\nbit longer before issuance to make sure tidal anomalies remain\non track with the current forecasts. Wind direction will play a\nkey role in this setup, and if it goes a bit more northerly,\nthat could drastically reduce the anomaly values, so there is\nstill some level of uncertainty at this time. \n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CJL/CPB/SRT\nAVIATION...CJL/CPB/SRT\nMARINE...CJL/CPB/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788714103,"days":{"2026-09-06":{"hi":85,"lo":65,"pop":4,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-07":{"hi":84,"lo":63,"pop":2,"short":"Patchy Fog then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-08":{"hi":86,"lo":66,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-09":{"hi":90,"lo":74,"pop":4,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-10":{"hi":90,"lo":68,"pop":40,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-11":{"hi":83,"lo":64,"pop":9,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-12":{"hi":85,"lo":67,"pop":10,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1788714000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,9900,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[7,7,7,7,7,6,5,4,4,4,3,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,3,4,4,5,5,5,5,5,5,4,4,3,2,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,2,2,3,3,3,4,4,4],"gst":[10,10,10,10,9,9,8,9,9,8,8,7,6,5,5,5,5,6,6,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,6